 >= 2 && x <= 3) {y = 2*(x - 2);}\n  return(y);\n}\n\nreal funca(real x) {\n  return(func(2*x + 1));\n}\n\nfor (n = 1; n <= 5; ++n) {\n  graf[n] = new picture;\n  for (i = -8; i <= 8; ++i) {\n    draw(graf[n],(i,-8)--(i,8),gray(0.7));\n    draw(graf[n],(-8,i)--(8,i),gray(0.7));\n  }\n  draw(graf[n],(-8,0)--(8,0),Arrows(6));\n  draw(graf[n],(0,-8)--(0,8),Arrows(6));\n\n  label(graf[n],\"$x$\", (8,0), E);\n  label(graf[n],\"$y$\", (0,8), N);\n}\n\ndraw(graf[1],graph(funca,-2,1),red);\ndraw(graf[2],shift((1,0))*xscale(2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[3],shift((1/2,0))*xscale(1/2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[4],shift((-1,0))*xscale(1/2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[5],shift((-1,0))*xscale(2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\n\nlabel(graf[1], \"A\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[2], \"B\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[3], \"C\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[4], \"D\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[5], \"E\", (0,-10));\n\nadd(graf[1]);\nadd(shift((20,0))*(graf[2]));\nadd(shift((40,0))*(graf[3]));\nadd(shift((10,-20))*(graf[4]));\nadd(shift((30,-20))*(graf[5]));\n[/asy]\n\nEnter the letter of the graph of $y = f(2x + 1).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b84643a014ad5cd0a125540eb0c05a5d1eb76f04d22980990a38d175512898dd"},"seconds":29.328032970428467,"seed":838563938469719785,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve this problem, we must understand the transformation applied to the function $y = f(x)$ to get $y = f(2x + 1)$ and identify the correct graph among the given options.\n\n1. **Transformation Explanation:**\n   - The transformation $y = f(2x + 1)$ involves both a horizontal compression and a horizontal shift.\n   - The term $2x$ implies a horizontal compression by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$, meaning that the graph is squeezed horizontally to half its original width.\n   - The term $+1$ inside the function argument implies a horizontal shift to the left by $\\frac{1}{2}$ unit, because to align the new function with the original, we must shift by $-\\frac{1}{2}$ of the compression factor $2$.\n\n2. **Identifying the Correct Graph:**\n   - Option A: This graph appears to be a horizontally compressed version of $y = f(x)$ and shifted to the left. It seems to match the transformation $y = f(2x + 1)$.\n   - Option B: This graph appears to be a horizontally stretched version (by a factor of 2) and shifted to the right, which does not match the transformation.\n   - Option C: This graph appears to be a horizontally compressed version (by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$) but shifted to the right, which does not match the transformation.\n   - Option D: This graph appears to be a horizontally compressed version (by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$) and shifted to the left, which matches the transformation.\n   - Option E: This graph appears to be a horizontally stretched version (by a factor of 2) and shifted to the left, which does not match the transformation.\n\nGiven the analysis, the graph that matches $y = f(2x + 1)$ is the one that is both horizontally compressed by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and shifted to the left by $\\frac{1}{2}$ unit.\n\nThus, the correct graph is \\(\\boxed{D}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{D}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
